<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VI was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1711 to 1740 and the last male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> of the senior line. A claimant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, he received instead the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1713–1714, and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1718. He created the Principality of Liechtenstein in 1719 and spent years winning acceptance of the Pragmatic Sanction for his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Gottfried Auerbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottfried_Auerbach_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna, Prague and later Pest grew as court, administrative and commercial centres, and <b>Charles VI</b> declared Trieste and Fiume free ports in 1719 to open an outlet to the Adriatic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 23 January 1719 Emperor <b>Charles VI</b> united the two and raised them to an imperial principality named Liechtenstein for Prince Anton Florian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of the Spanish Succession, the Treaties of Utrecht and Rastatt in 1713–1714 transferred the Spanish Netherlands to <b>Emperor Charles VI</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a></li><li><blockquote>Rather than returning it to Hungary, <b>Charles VI</b> kept it as a crown domain under military and court administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1719 <b>Emperor Charles VI</b> united the two as the Principality of Liechtenstein, of which Vaduz is the capital today.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vaduz">County of Vaduz</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1719 <b>Emperor Charles VI</b> united Schellenberg and Vaduz as the Principality of Liechtenstein, where it survives as the Unterland.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-schellenberg">Lordship of Schellenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1720" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1720">What was here in 1720? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
